function twoSum(nums, target) {
  const map = new Map();
  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    map.set(nums[i], i);                 // <-- added too early
    const complement = target - nums[i];
    if (map.has(complement)) {
      return [map.get(complement), i];
    }
  }
  return null;
}

model.en:
Storing <code>nums[i]</code> before the lookup lets an element pair with <em>itself</em>. For <code>twoSum([3, 5], 6)</code> the code returns <code>[0, 0]</code> — index 0 used twice — because <code>3</code> is already in the map when its own complement <code>3</code> is checked. Fix: check the complement first, and only then add the current number:
<pre>const complement = target - nums[i];
if (map.has(complement)) return [map.get(complement), i];
map.set(nums[i], i);</pre>
This preserves the seen-pattern invariant: the map holds only <em>earlier</em> elements when you probe it.

